LEARN.technique({id:"alternate-picking",name:"Alternate picking",family:"Lead",level:"Beginner",instr:"guitar",summary:"Alternate picking means strictly alternating downstrokes and upstrokes in a continuous run. It is the most common way to play scale runs and fast single-note lines with a pick, and at very high tempos it is close to essential.",how:["Start on one string with a metronome. Play downstrokes on the beats and upstrokes on the 'and's, counting '1 and 2 and', then fill in sixteenths once that feels even.","Keep the motion small: only the tip of the pick should meet the string, and the movement should come mostly from the wrist. Grips and motions vary between great players (Paul Gilbert picks mainly from the wrist, Jason Becker more from the fingers), so aim for relaxed and consistent.","Keep the down-up chain going when you change strings. With an even number of notes per string, every string starts on the same stroke. With three notes per string, the pick direction flips on each new string, which is the hard part to drill.","Practise both kinds of string change: outside picking (the pick crosses to the next string around the outside of the pair) and inside picking (the pick works between the two strings). Most players find outside picking easier at first.","Accent the first note of each group of four sixteenths. This locks your two hands together and makes timing problems easy to hear.","Raise the tempo only when the pattern is clean, in small steps of around 10 bpm. Spend some practice time near your top speed, but most of it at a speed you can control."],mistakes:["A stiff, tense picking hand. Keep the hand loose and light: tension makes notes less articulate and tires you quickly.","A big picking motion that clips neighbouring strings. Keep the pick close to the string you are playing.","Losing time at string changes. Slow the exercise down until the change sits exactly on the grid, then build back up.","Pushing the metronome before the pattern is clean, which trains sloppy timing into your hands."],used:"Everywhere from jazz and bluegrass to heavy metal. Al Di Meola, Steve Morse and Jeff Hanneman are known for picking almost every note this way, and Paul Gilbert, John Petrucci and Yngwie Malmsteen are common reference points for fast picked lines. Palm-muted alternate picking drives many thrash and death metal rhythm parts.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Cram Session: Alternate Picking",author:"Jamie Humphries",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2015,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/cram-session-alternate-picking",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"Alternate picking",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternate_picking",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s3",title:"Guitar/Alternate Picking",site:"Wikibooks",url:"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Guitar/Alternate_Picking",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"Alternate Picking Lesson",site:"fretjam",url:"https://fretjam.com/alternate-picking.html",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s5",title:"10 Steps to Master Alternate Picking Technique For Guitar",author:"Rob Thorpe",site:"Fundamental Changes",url:"https://www.fundamental-changes.com/guitar-alternate-picking-technique/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s6",title:"Want to Improve Your Alternate Picking? This Essential Lesson Will Have You Shredding in No Time",site:"Guitar Player",url:"https://www.guitarplayer.com/lessons/alternate-picking-the-ups-and-downs-of-an-essential-technique",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"'Intense Rock' Picking: A Brief Look at Paul Gilbert's Alternate Picking Technique",kind:"article",url:"https://guitarworld.com/lessons/intense-rock-picking-a-brief-look-at-paul-gilberts-alternate-picking-technique",note:"Shows what happens to the pick at string changes in one of the best-known picking videos."},{title:"Al Di Meola's alternate picking (Guitar World lesson)",kind:"lesson",url:"https://guitarworld.com/lessons/al-di-meola-alternate-picking-fusion-shred",note:"Hand synchronisation and rhythmic phrasing from a player who picks nearly every note."},{title:"5 alternate picking exercises that will speed up your guitar playing",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/alternate-picking-technique-workout",note:"A compact daily workout once the exercises here feel comfortable."},{title:"Cracking the Code",author:"Troy Grady",kind:"video",note:"A long-running video series that films and analyses how fast pickers actually move the pick, useful once you hit a speed wall."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5","s6"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"alternate-picking-one-string",title:"One string: eighths, then sixteenths",instr:"guitar",bpm:80,grid:4,notes:[[0,1,5,2,"D"],[2,1,8,2,"U"],[4,1,7,2,"D"],[6,1,5,2,"U"],[8,1,8,2,"D"],[10,1,10,2,"U"],[12,1,8,2,"D"],[14,1,7,2,"U"],[16,1,5,1,"D"],[17,1,7,1,"U"],[18,1,8,1,"D"],[19,1,7,1,"U"],[20,1,5,1,"D"],[21,1,7,1,"U"],[22,1,8,1,"D"],[23,1,10,1,"U"],[24,1,12,1,"D"],[25,1,10,1,"U"],[26,1,8,1,"D"],[27,1,7,1,"U"],[28,1,5,4,"D"]],note:"An A minor line on the top string. Downstrokes land on the beats in bar 1, then the hand simply moves twice as often in bar 2.",cites:["s4"]},{type:"tab",id:"alternate-picking-two-per-string",title:"Two notes per string across the neck",instr:"guitar",bpm:72,grid:4,notes:[[0,6,5,1,"D"],[1,6,8,1,"U"],[2,5,5,1,"D"],[3,5,7,1,"U"],[4,4,5,1,"D"],[5,4,7,1,"U"],[6,3,5,1,"D"],[7,3,7,1,"U"],[8,2,5,1,"D"],[9,2,8,1,"U"],[10,1,5,1,"D"],[11,1,8,1,"U"],[12,1,8,1,"D"],[13,1,5,1,"U"],[14,2,8,1,"D"],[15,2,5,1,"U"],[16,3,7,1,"D"],[17,3,5,1,"U"],[18,4,7,1,"D"],[19,4,5,1,"U"],[20,5,7,1,"D"],[21,5,5,1,"U"],[22,6,8,1,"D"],[23,6,5,1,"U"],[24,4,7,8,"D"]],note:"The A minor pentatonic box in sixteenths. Every string starts on a downstroke, so you only have one kind of string change to master here.",cites:["s1","s5"]},{type:"tab",id:"alternate-picking-three-per-string",title:"Three notes per string: the stroke flips",instr:"guitar",bpm:72,grid:4,notes:[[0,5,5,1,"D"],[1,5,7,1,"U"],[2,5,8,1,"D"],[3,4,5,1,"U"],[4,4,7,1,"D"],[5,4,9,1,"U"],[6,3,5,1,"D"],[7,3,7,1,"U"],[8,3,9,1,"D"],[9,2,6,1,"U"],[10,2,8,1,"D"],[11,2,10,1,"U"],[12,1,7,1,"D"],[13,1,8,1,"U"],[14,1,10,1,"D"],[15,1,12,1,"U"],[16,1,10,1,"D"],[17,1,8,1,"U"],[18,1,7,1,"D"],[19,2,10,1,"U"],[20,2,8,1,"D"],[21,2,6,1,"U"],[22,3,9,1,"D"],[23,3,7,1,"U"],[24,3,5,1,"D"],[25,4,9,1,"U"],[26,4,7,1,"D"],[27,4,5,1,"U"],[28,5,8,1,"D"],[29,5,7,1,"U"],[30,5,5,1,"D"],[31,6,8,1,"U"],[32,6,5,8,"D"]],note:"A minor in three-notes-per-string shapes. Each new string starts on the opposite stroke from the last one: watch the D and U marks and keep the accents on the beat.",cites:["s1","s6"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"economy-picking",name:"Economy picking",family:"Lead",level:"Intermediate",instr:"guitar",summary:"Economy picking mixes alternate picking and sweep picking. You alternate while you stay on one string, and when you move to the next string you pick in the direction you are already travelling, so the pick never has to jump back over a string.",how:["On a single string, alternate down and up as usual.","When the next note is on a higher-pitched (thinner) string, play it with a downstroke. When it is on a lower-pitched (thicker) string, play it with an upstroke. That string change becomes a tiny two-note sweep.","Let the pick come to rest against the next string at the end of the stroke, then push through it in the same motion. Two strokes in the same direction should feel like one movement.","Learn it first on three-notes-per-string scales. Ascending, the picking reads D U D, D U D on every string. Descending, each string starts with an upstroke: U D U, U D U.","Play slowly at first and check your timing with a metronome. The swept pair must land on the grid just like every other note."],mistakes:["Rushing the swept pair so the two notes flam together. Keep every note evenly spaced in time, even when two strokes go the same way.","Losing articulation. Economy picking can sound less defined than strict alternate picking, so dig in evenly with each stroke and listen back to a recording.","Applying it where nothing changes. With an even number of notes per string, economy picking and alternate picking give the same strokes, so the sweeps only appear with odd groupings."],used:"Fusion and rock players who need speed with less motion. Frank Gambale wrote an early dedicated book on it and mixes alternate and sweep picking in his runs. Les Paul is credited with an early form of it, and Shrapnel-era players such as Yngwie Malmsteen and Vinnie Moore popularised alternate-picked scales with swept arpeggios. Eric Johnson and Zakk Wylde are among its users, and gypsy jazz picking uses a related idea with rest strokes at string changes.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Economy picking",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economy_picking",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"The Art of Economy Picking",author:"Rusty Cooley",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2009,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/the-art-of-economy-picking",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s3",title:"Cram Session: Sweep Picking",author:"Jamie Humphries",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2016,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/cram-session-sweep-picking",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"How to use economy picking: a beginner's guide to an expert technique",site:"Guitar World",url:"https://guitarworld.com/lessons/how-to-use-economy-picking",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s5",title:"What is economy picking and how can it benefit your guitar playing?",site:"Guitar World",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/economy-picking-made-easy-part-1",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s6",title:"Demystifying Shred: How to Achieve Maximum Speed with Minimal Effort",site:"Guitar Player",url:"https://www.guitarplayer.com/lessons/demystifying-shred-how-to-achieve-maximum-speed-with-minimal-effort",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Speed Picking",author:"Frank Gambale",kind:"book",note:"An early, thorough method book built entirely around this picking system."},{title:"Monster Licks & Speed Picking",author:"Frank Gambale",kind:"video",note:"The 1988 instructional video where Gambale demonstrates the approach at full speed."},{title:"Economy picking made easy, part 2",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/economy-picking-made-easy-part-2",note:"More exercises that move from scales to arpeggios."},{title:"Economy Picking 101",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarmasterclass.net/ls/Economy-Picking-101",note:"A free video lesson that starts from the basic string-change rule."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5","s6"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"economy-picking-scale",title:"G major, three notes per string, up and down",instr:"guitar",bpm:72,grid:3,notes:[[0,6,3,1,"D"],[1,6,5,1,"U"],[2,6,7,1,"D"],[3,5,3,1,"D"],[4,5,5,1,"U"],[5,5,7,1,"D"],[6,4,4,1,"D"],[7,4,5,1,"U"],[8,4,7,1,"D"],[9,3,4,1,"D"],[10,3,5,1,"U"],[11,3,7,1,"D"],[12,2,8,1,"D"],[13,2,7,1,"U"],[14,2,5,1,"D"],[15,3,7,1,"U"],[16,3,5,1,"D"],[17,3,4,1,"U"],[18,4,7,1,"U"],[19,4,5,1,"D"],[20,4,4,1,"U"],[21,5,7,1,"U"],[22,5,5,1,"D"],[23,5,3,1,"U"],[24,6,3,12,"U"]],note:"Going up, every string change is two downstrokes in a row (D then D). Coming down, from the G string onward every change is two upstrokes in a row. Make each pair one smooth push of the pick.",cites:["s1","s2"]},{type:"tab",id:"economy-picking-arpeggios",title:"Arpeggio turnarounds: sweep down, sweep back",instr:"guitar",bpm:66,grid:3,notes:[[0,4,7,1,"D"],[1,3,5,1,"D"],[2,2,5,1,"D"],[3,1,5,1,"D"],[4,1,8,1,"U"],[5,1,5,1,"D"],[6,2,5,1,"U"],[7,3,5,1,"U"],[8,4,7,4,"U"],[12,4,5,1,"D"],[13,3,4,1,"D"],[14,2,3,1,"D"],[15,1,3,1,"D"],[16,1,7,1,"U"],[17,1,3,1,"D"],[18,2,3,1,"U"],[19,3,4,1,"U"],[20,4,5,4,"U"]],note:"Am then G. One note per string means one long stroke across them; on the top string you alternate, then sweep back up. Let each note stop as the next one sounds so the arpeggio never rings like a chord.",cites:["s3","s6"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"sweep-picking",name:"Sweep picking",family:"Lead",level:"Advanced",instr:"guitar",summary:"Sweep picking plays one note per string across adjacent strings with a single, continuous stroke: down as you go toward the high string, up as you come back. It is the standard way to play fast arpeggios, and the fretting hand has to make each note sound on its own.",how:["Start small. Learn a two-string or three-string shape first, then add strings until you can cover five. Move on only when the smaller shape is clean.","Ascending, use downstrokes; descending, use upstrokes. After each string the pick comes to rest on the next string, so the whole shape is one smooth motion of the hand.","Fret one note at a time and lift each finger slightly just after its note sounds. This stops the arpeggio from blurring into a strummed chord.","Turn the shape around on the top string with a hammer-on and pull-off, so the pick does not have to change direction mid-string.","Keep both hands relaxed, especially in wider shapes, and play slowly enough that every note is even in time and volume."],mistakes:["Notes ringing together like a chord. Lift each fretting finger slightly as soon as the next note sounds.","Treating the sweep as a strum. The pick should arrive at each string in rhythm with the fretting hand, one clean note at a time, so start slowly and keep the motion smooth.","Tension in stretched shapes. Stay loose in the fretting hand and slow down until the stretch feels easy."],used:"Jazz guitarists including Les Paul, Chet Atkins, Tal Farlow and Barney Kessel used it in the 1950s, rock players such as Jan Akkerman, Ritchie Blackmore and Steve Hackett in the 1970s, and 1980s shred players made it famous. Frank Gambale, Yngwie Malmsteen, Jason Becker and Richie Kotzen are well-known sweepers.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Sweep picking",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweep_picking",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"Cram Session: Sweep Picking",author:"Jamie Humphries",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2016,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/cram-session-sweep-picking",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s3",title:"The Shred Decoder's Guide to Sweep Picking",author:"Al Joseph",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2024,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/the-shred-decoders-guide-to-sweep-picking",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"Sweep Picking",author:"Mike Campese",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2008,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/sweep-picking",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s5",title:"Guitar picking",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guitar_picking",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s6",title:"3 Essential Sweep Picking Shapes",author:"DJ Temple",site:"GuitarLessons.com",url:"https://guitarlessons.com/guitar-lessons/lead-guitar/3-essential-sweep-picking-shapes/",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Monster Licks & Speed Picking",author:"Frank Gambale",kind:"video",note:"The 1988 video that brought sweeping to a generation of players, with the picking explained slowly."},{title:"Nita Strauss shows you how to sweep pick",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/sweep-picking-with-nita-strauss",note:"A modern rock player walking through her own sweep shapes."},{title:"Sweep picking: how to get started with this awe-inspiring guitar technique",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/sweep-picking-how-to-get-started",note:"Builds from two strings up to six at one metronome tempo."},{title:"Straten Marshall sweep picking lessons",kind:"course",url:"https://jamplay.com/guitar-teachers/168-straten-marshall",note:"A video series that covers major, minor and diminished three-string shapes."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5","s6"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"tip",text:"Mike Campese calls sweeping 'a similar technique to raking'. The difference that matters: in a sweep, every string the pick crosses carries a fretted note that should sound clearly on its own.",cites:["s4","s5"]},{type:"tab",id:"sweep-picking-three-string",title:"Three-string shapes: Am, G, F, E",instr:"guitar",bpm:60,grid:4,notes:[[0,3,14,1,"D"],[1,2,13,1,"D"],[2,1,12,1,"D"],[3,1,17,1,"h"],[4,1,12,1,"p"],[5,2,13,1,"U"],[6,3,14,2,"U"],[8,3,12,1,"D"],[9,2,12,1,"D"],[10,1,10,1,"D"],[11,1,15,1,"h"],[12,1,10,1,"p"],[13,2,12,1,"U"],[14,3,12,2,"U"],[16,3,10,1,"D"],[17,2,10,1,"D"],[18,1,8,1,"D"],[19,1,13,1,"h"],[20,1,8,1,"p"],[21,2,10,1,"U"],[22,3,10,2,"U"],[24,3,9,1,"D"],[25,2,9,1,"D"],[26,1,7,1,"D"],[27,1,12,1,"h"],[28,1,7,1,"p"],[29,2,9,1,"U"],[30,3,9,2,"U"],[32,3,14,8,"D,v"]],note:"One downstroke across three strings, a hammer-on and pull-off on the top string, then one upstroke back. The chords walk down A minor (Am, G, F, E) and resolve to A.",cites:["s1","s2","s4"]},{type:"tab",id:"sweep-picking-five-string",title:"Five-string shapes: Am, G, F, E",instr:"guitar",bpm:56,grid:3,notes:[[0,5,12,1,"D"],[1,4,10,1,"D"],[2,3,9,1,"D"],[3,2,10,1,"D"],[4,1,8,1,"D"],[5,1,12,1,"h"],[6,1,8,1,"p"],[7,2,10,1,"U"],[8,3,9,1,"U"],[9,4,10,1,"U"],[10,5,12,2,"U"],[12,5,10,1,"D"],[13,4,9,1,"D"],[14,3,7,1,"D"],[15,2,8,1,"D"],[16,1,7,1,"D"],[17,1,10,1,"h"],[18,1,7,1,"p"],[19,2,8,1,"U"],[20,3,7,1,"U"],[21,4,9,1,"U"],[22,5,10,2,"U"],[24,5,8,1,"D"],[25,4,7,1,"D"],[26,3,5,1,"D"],[27,2,6,1,"D"],[28,1,5,1,"D"],[29,1,8,1,"h"],[30,1,5,1,"p"],[31,2,6,1,"U"],[32,3,5,1,"U"],[33,4,7,1,"U"],[34,5,8,2,"U"],[36,5,7,1,"D"],[37,4,6,1,"D"],[38,3,4,1,"D"],[39,2,5,1,"D"],[40,1,4,1,"D"],[41,1,7,1,"h"],[42,1,4,1,"p"],[43,2,5,1,"U"],[44,3,4,1,"U"],[45,4,6,1,"U"],[46,5,7,2,"U"]],note:"The same progression with root-on-the-A-string shapes, two octaves each. Lift every finger as the next note sounds; loop the four bars once it is clean.",cites:["s3","s4","s6"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"tapping",name:"Two-hand tapping",family:"Lead",level:"Intermediate",instr:"guitar",summary:"Tapping uses a finger of your picking hand to hammer notes onto the fretboard, usually linked with pull-offs and hammer-ons from the fretting hand. It gives smooth legato lines and wide intervals that one hand cannot reach, and with both hands tapping it lets you play the guitar a bit like a keyboard.",how:["Fret a note with your fretting hand, then tap firmly with the tip of a picking-hand finger higher up the same string. If you keep holding the pick, tap with your middle finger; Dylan Mattheisen's quick middle-finger taps are a good example.","Release the tap by pulling the finger slightly sideways, toward the floor or toward your head, so it plucks the string and sounds the note your fretting hand is holding.","Hammer on with the fretting hand to sound the next note, then tap again. Tap, pull-off, hammer-on in a steady triplet is the classic rock pattern.","Keep idle strings quiet with parts of both hands. Some players also put a hair tie, foam or a fret wrap near the nut when both hands tap.","For clean two-hand playing, let the picking hand's index and middle fingers take the upper notes while the fretting hand hammers the lower ones, so the parts interlock like a pianist's two hands.","Start at about half speed (around 80 bpm) and work up gradually."],mistakes:["Lifting the tapping finger straight up. Pull it slightly sideways so it plucks the string and the fretted note speaks.","Unwanted string noise, which gets louder with gain. Mute every string you are not using, with both hands.","Uneven volume between tapped, pulled and hammered notes. Tapped notes have a narrower range of tone, so many players use a compressor and practise until each note matches."],used:"Eddie Van Halen made it famous in the late 1970s, and 'Eruption' brought two-hand tapping to a mainstream rock audience; earlier tappers include Harvey Mandel, Steve Hackett and Emmett Chapman. Stanley Jordan is known for piano-like two-handed playing, and Joe Satriani's 'Midnight' is a clean tapped piece. Math rock guitarists use tapping, often in alternate tunings, to build its intricate parts, and the style overlaps with Midwest emo bands such as American Football and Tiny Moving Parts; Covet's Yvette Young and Tiny Moving Parts' Dylan Mattheisen are well-known tappers in that scene.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Tapping",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tapping",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"Eruption (instrumental)",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eruption_(instrumental)",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s3",title:"How to Tap the Pentatonic Scale on the Guitar",author:"Desi Serna",site:"Dummies",url:"https://dummies.com/article/how-to-tap-the-pentatonic-scale-on-the-guitar-143488",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"Covet's Yvette Young Breaks Free",author:"Joe Charupakorn",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2023,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/artists/guitarists/yvette-young-breaks-free",kind:"interview"},{id:"s5",title:"Watch Yvette Young teach her transformative tapping technique in this new video lesson",site:"Guitar World",url:"https://guitarworld.com/news/yvette-young-guitar-tapping-lesson",kind:"video"},{id:"s6",title:"Think emo guitarists can't shred? Witness Tiny Moving Parts' hook-laden tapping techniques in this insanely catchy guitar playthrough",site:"Guitar World",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/news/think-emo-guitarists-cant-shred-witness-tiny-moving-parts-hook-laden-tapping-techniques-in-this-insanely-catchy-guitar-playthrough",kind:"video"},{id:"s7",title:"The Guitar Influences of Eddie Van Halen",author:"Justin Beckner",site:"Guitar.com",year:2025,url:"https://guitar.com/features/music/the-guitar-influences-of-eddie-van-halen/",kind:"article"},{id:"s8",title:"Future Rock: Two-Handed Tapping and How to Play the Intro to 'Atlas Novus'",author:"Chris Letchford",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2013,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/articles/Future_Rock_Two_Handed_Tapping_and_How_to_Play_the_Intro_to_Atlas_Novus",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Van Halen – Van Halen",kind:"album",note:"The debut album with 'Eruption', the tapping showcase that changed rock guitar."},{title:"Covet – Technicolor",kind:"album",note:"Modern clean two-hand tapping in a math rock setting from Yvette Young."},{title:"Dylan Mattheisen turns 'Born to Run' into a two-hand tapping, altered-tuning piece",kind:"video",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/news/dylan-mattheisen-transforms-bruce-springsteens-born-to-run-into-a-two-hand-tapping-altered-tuning-midwest-emo-masterpiece",note:"Shows how a Midwest emo player arranges a whole song for two hands."},{title:"Yvette Young teaches guitar finger tapping",kind:"lesson",url:"https://musicradar.com/news/yvette-young-guitar-tapping-lesson",note:"Covers strings, action and effects as well as the steps to build tapping technique."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5","s6","s7","s8"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"tapping-triplets",title:"Rock tap triplets on one string",instr:"guitar",bpm:100,grid:3,notes:[[0,1,12,1,"t"],[1,1,5,1,"p"],[2,1,8,1,"h"],[3,1,12,1,"t"],[4,1,5,1,"p"],[5,1,8,1,"h"],[6,1,13,1,"t"],[7,1,5,1,"p"],[8,1,8,1,"h"],[9,1,13,1,"t"],[10,1,5,1,"p"],[11,1,8,1,"h"],[12,1,15,1,"t"],[13,1,7,1,"p"],[14,1,10,1,"h"],[15,1,15,1,"t"],[16,1,7,1,"p"],[17,1,10,1,"h"],[18,1,12,1,"t"],[19,1,4,1,"p"],[20,1,7,1,"h"],[21,1,12,1,"t"],[22,1,4,1,"p"],[23,1,7,1,"h"],[24,1,12,1,"t"],[25,1,8,1,"p"],[26,1,5,4,"p,v"]],note:"Tap, pull-off, hammer-on: each beat spells a triad (Am, F, G, E) on the high E string. Play it with some gain and keep the pull-offs as loud as the taps.",cites:["s1","s2","s3"]},{type:"tab",id:"tapping-across-strings",title:"Moving the tap triplet across strings",instr:"guitar",bpm:90,grid:3,notes:[[0,1,12,1,"t"],[1,1,5,1,"p"],[2,1,8,1,"h"],[3,2,13,1,"t"],[4,2,5,1,"p"],[5,2,8,1,"h"],[6,3,14,1,"t"],[7,3,5,1,"p"],[8,3,9,1,"h"],[9,2,12,1,"t"],[10,2,5,1,"p"],[11,2,9,1,"h"],[12,1,12,1,"t"],[13,1,5,1,"p"],[14,1,8,1,"h"],[15,1,12,1,"t"],[16,1,8,1,"p"],[17,1,5,7,"p,v"]],note:"Am on the E string, C on the B string, Am on the G string, E on the B string, then home. Mute the strings you are not tapping, because the pattern now crosses strings.",cites:["s3"]},{type:"tab",id:"tapping-two-hand-clean",title:"Clean two-hand arpeggios (math rock and emo)",instr:"guitar",bpm:100,grid:4,ring:!0,notes:[[0,5,3,4,"h"],[1,4,5,4,"h"],[2,2,13,4,"t"],[3,1,12,4,"t"],[4,5,3,4,"h"],[5,4,5,4,"h"],[6,2,13,4,"t"],[7,1,15,4,"t"],[8,5,3,4,"h"],[9,4,5,4,"h"],[10,2,12,4,"t"],[11,1,12,4,"t"],[12,5,3,4,"h"],[13,4,5,4,"h"],[14,2,12,4,"t"],[15,1,10,4,"t"],[16,4,3,4,"h"],[17,3,5,4,"h"],[18,2,10,4,"t"],[19,1,12,4,"t"],[20,4,3,4,"h"],[21,3,5,4,"h"],[22,2,10,4,"t"],[23,1,15,4,"t"],[24,4,3,4,"h"],[25,3,5,4,"h"],[26,2,10,4,"t"],[27,1,13,4,"t"],[28,4,3,4,"h"],[29,3,5,4,"h"],[30,2,10,4,"t"],[31,1,12,4,"t"]],note:"Cmaj7 to Fmaj7. The fretting hand hammers two low notes from nowhere, the picking hand taps two high notes, and everything rings. Keep it clean and even, like a broken chord on a piano.",cites:["s1","s3","s4"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"legato",name:"Hammer-ons and pull-offs",family:"Lead",level:"Intermediate",instr:"guitar",summary:"Legato playing sounds most notes with the fretting hand, using hammer-ons, pull-offs and slides, so a line flows smoothly with very few pick strokes. The same moves give you trills and the long three-notes-per-string runs of rock and fusion soloists.",how:["Hammer-on: pick a note, then bring another finger down sharply just behind a higher fret so the new note sounds without picking. Use fingertips, one finger per fret, and keep your fingers close to the strings.","Pull-off: with both notes fretted, pull the higher finger off with a slight sideways flick so it plucks the string and the lower note sounds. Dragging the finger a little across the string adds volume and sustain.","Trill: alternate a hammer-on and pull-off between two notes as fast and evenly as you can. Practising each pair of fingers (1-2, 1-3, 1-4, 2-3 and so on) evens out their strength.","Three notes per string: pick the first note on each string and hammer the next two going up. Coming down, start each new string with a 'hammer-on from nowhere' (fret it hard enough to sound without a pick) and pull off from there.","Pick lightly so the picked note is no louder than the slurred ones. Mute the strings you are not using with the heel of your picking hand and with your fretting-hand index finger."],mistakes:["A loud picked note followed by weak slurred notes. Pick softer and hammer harder until every note matches.","Open strings ringing between notes. Keep both hands muting as you change strings.","A weak little finger that drops notes. Trill exercises with every finger pair build the strength you need.","Picking more often than the line needs, which breaks the flow."],used:"Allan Holdsworth is the model many legato players point to, and Steve Vai's 1984 lesson recommends him as a master of the style. Joe Satriani builds diatonic legato lines from three-notes-per-string shapes ('Flying in a Blue Dream'), and Steve Vai and Eddie Van Halen used it all over their solos. The names 'hammer-on' and 'pull-off' themselves are credited to the folk musician Pete Seeger.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Shred Like Satriani: A Crash Course in Modern Legato",author:"Jamie Humphries",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2016,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/shred/joe-satriani-legato-guitar-lesson",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"Pull-Off Playing (incl. Hammer-Ons)",author:"Steve Vai",site:"vai.com",year:1984,url:"https://www.vai.com/pull-off-playing-incl-hammer-ons/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s3",title:"How To Play Legato On Guitar",author:"Giancarlo Facoetti",site:"FaChords",url:"https://www.fachords.com/legato/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"Hammer-on",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hammer-on",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s5",title:"Pull-off",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pull-off",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s6",title:"Step Up Your Legato Playing With These 3 Guitar Lessons From Chris Buono",site:"TrueFire Blog",year:2020,url:"https://blog.truefire.com/?p=14846",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Joe Satriani – Flying in a Blue Dream",kind:"album",note:"The title track is a textbook of short legato bursts against long held notes."},{title:"Learn 60 years of legato guitar in 5 generation-spanning lessons",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/legato-evolution-lesson",note:"Traces how legato changed from one generation of players to the next."},{title:"Allen Hinds: rediscover the fretboard with these 8 legato ideas",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarplayer.com/lessons/allen-hinds-rediscover-the-fretboard-with-these-8-legato-ideas",note:"Fusion-flavoured legato lines from a session player."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5","s6"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"legato-pairs",title:"Pick-hammer up, pick-pull down",instr:"guitar",bpm:80,grid:4,notes:[[0,4,5,2,"D"],[2,4,7,2,"h"],[4,3,5,2,"D"],[6,3,7,2,"h"],[8,2,5,2,"D"],[10,2,8,2,"h"],[12,1,5,2,"D"],[14,1,8,2,"h"],[16,1,8,2,"D"],[18,1,5,2,"p"],[20,2,8,2,"D"],[22,2,5,2,"p"],[24,3,7,2,"D"],[26,3,5,2,"p"],[28,4,7,4,"D,v"]],note:"A minor pentatonic in eighth notes: pick one, slur one. Aim for the slurred note to be as loud as the picked one.",cites:["s3","s4","s5"]},{type:"tab",id:"legato-trills",title:"Trills on three strings",instr:"guitar",bpm:72,grid:4,notes:[[0,3,5,1,"D"],[1,3,7,1,"h"],[2,3,5,1,"p"],[3,3,7,1,"h"],[4,3,5,1,"p"],[5,3,7,1,"h"],[6,3,5,1,"p"],[7,3,7,1,"h"],[8,2,5,1,"D"],[9,2,6,1,"h"],[10,2,5,1,"p"],[11,2,6,1,"h"],[12,2,5,1,"p"],[13,2,6,1,"h"],[14,2,5,2,"p"],[16,1,7,1,"D"],[17,1,8,1,"h"],[18,1,7,1,"p"],[19,1,8,1,"h"],[20,1,7,1,"p"],[21,1,8,1,"h"],[22,1,7,1,"p"],[23,1,5,9,"p,v"]],note:"One pick stroke per trill. The C-D trill uses a whole step, the E-F and B-C trills a half step; the last pull-off drops to A.",cites:["s2","s4"]},{type:"tab",id:"legato-three-per-string",title:"Three-notes-per-string legato run",instr:"guitar",bpm:84,grid:3,notes:[[0,4,7,1,"D"],[1,4,9,1,"h"],[2,4,10,1,"h"],[3,3,7,1,"D"],[4,3,9,1,"h"],[5,3,10,1,"h"],[6,2,8,1,"D"],[7,2,10,1,"h"],[8,2,12,1,"h"],[9,1,8,1,"D"],[10,1,10,1,"h"],[11,1,12,1,"h"],[12,1,12,1,"D"],[13,1,10,1,"p"],[14,1,8,1,"p"],[15,2,12,1,"h"],[16,2,10,1,"p"],[17,2,8,1,"p"],[18,3,10,1,"h"],[19,3,9,1,"p"],[20,3,7,1,"p"],[21,4,10,1,"h"],[22,4,9,1,"p"],[23,4,7,13,"p,v"]],note:"A natural minor, two octaves. Going up you pick once per string; coming down you only pick the first note and start every other string with a hammer-on from nowhere.",cites:["s1","s6"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"bends",name:"String bending",family:"Lead",level:"Beginner",instr:"guitar",summary:"Bending pushes or pulls a fretted string sideways to raise its pitch. A half-step bend raises the note by one fret's worth, a whole step by two, and a good bend lands exactly on its target note.",how:["Before you bend, fret the target note and listen to it. Then go back two frets (for a whole step) or one fret (for a half step) and bend until the pitch matches.","Bend with your ring finger and put your middle and index fingers on the same string behind it for support. Let the wrist do the work, turning like a motorbike throttle, with the thumb hooked over the top of the neck for grip.","On the top three strings, push the string toward your head. On the low strings, pull it toward the floor so it does not slip off the edge of the fretboard.","Practise bend and release (bend up, then let it back down in time) and the pre-bend (bend silently, pick, then release so only the falling pitch is heard). Pre-bends are the hardest to get in tune, because you have to feel the pitch before you hear it.","Add bigger bends and unison bends once whole steps are reliable. A 1.5-step bend raises the note by three frets' worth. In a unison bend you play two strings together and bend the lower one until both sound the same pitch.","Mute the strings next to the bend so they do not ring as your fingers drag across them."],mistakes:["Stopping short of the target (bending flat). Play the target note again and compare.","Bending with finger strength alone, which makes the pitch sharp or wobbly and tires your forearm. Rotate the wrist and keep the fingers firm.","Using one unsupported finger, which feels weak and inconsistent. Add the supporting fingers behind it.","Letting the bending finger slide under the next string. Push against the neighbouring string and keep it on top of your finger."],used:"Blues, rock and country lead playing. T-Bone Walker, B.B. King, Eric Clapton, David Gilmour, Tony Iommi and Brian May are often named as masters of string bending.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"How to Bend Strings (Guitar Bend Types and Examples with Audio)",site:"JG Music Lessons",url:"https://jgmusiclessons.com/guitar-string-bending/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"Guitar String Bending Lesson for Beginners",site:"fretjam",url:"https://www.fretjam.com/string-bending.html",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s3",title:"How to Use Bends in Your Guitar Solo",author:"Jon Chappell, Mark Phillips, Desi Serna",site:"Dummies (Guitar All-in-One For Dummies)",url:"https://www.dummies.com/article/academics-the-arts/music/instruments/guitar/how-to-use-bends-in-your-guitar-solo-149151",kind:"book"},{id:"s4",title:"Guitar Bends Masterclass",site:"Guitar Alliance",url:"https://guitaralliance.net/guitar-bends/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s5",title:"Bending Strings: How To Bend Guitar Strings",site:"Active Melody",url:"https://www.activemelody.com/lesson/bending-strings-how-to-bend-guitar-strings/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s6",title:"Finger vibrato",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finger_vibrato",kind:"encyclopedia"}],study:[{title:"String-Bending Masterclass: How to Make Your Guitar Wail and Sing Like the Pros",kind:"lesson",url:"https://guitarplayer.com/lessons/string-bending-masterclass-how-to-make-your-guitar-wail-and-sing-like-the-pros",note:"A deeper tour of bend types and how the pros phrase them."},{title:"String Bending: The One-Hour Workout",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarplayer.com/technique/string-bending-the-one-hour-workout",note:"A structured practice session for pitch accuracy."},{title:"Guitar Bend Technique Overview",kind:"lesson",url:"https://hubguitar.com/technique/string-bending-overview",note:"Explains one-, two- and three-finger bends and when each makes sense."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5","s6"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"bends-in-tune",title:"Hear the target, then bend to it",instr:"guitar",bpm:76,grid:4,notes:[[0,3,9,4],[4,3,7,4,"b2"],[8,2,10,4],[12,2,8,4,"b2"],[16,1,8,4],[20,1,7,4,"b1"],[24,1,5,8,"v"]],note:"Each bend follows its fretted target: E then a whole-step bend from D, A then a whole step from G, C then a half step from B. The bent note should match the one before it exactly.",cites:["s1","s2"]},{type:"tab",id:"bends-release-and-big",title:"Pre-bend, half, whole and 1.5-step bends",instr:"guitar",bpm:72,grid:4,notes:[[0,2,8,4,"rb2"],[4,2,5,2],[6,3,7,2,"b1"],[8,2,10,8,"b3,v"],[16,1,8,2],[18,1,5,2],[20,2,8,4,"b2"],[24,2,5,2],[26,3,5,2],[28,4,7,4,"v"]],note:"An A minor blues phrase: a pre-bent G released, a half-step curl on D, a big 1.5-step bend from A up to C, then a whole-step bend in bar 2.",cites:["s1","s3","s4"]},{type:"tab",id:"bends-unison",title:"Unison bends",instr:"guitar",bpm:84,grid:4,notes:[[0,3,7,4,"b2"],[0,2,5,4],[4,3,7,4,"b2"],[4,2,5,4],[8,2,8,8,"b2,v"],[8,1,5,8],[16,3,7,2,"b2"],[16,2,5,2],[18,3,7,2,"b2"],[18,2,5,2],[20,2,8,4,"b2"],[20,1,5,4],[24,4,7,8,"v"]],note:"Bend the lower string up a whole step until it matches the note held on the string above. You will hear the two pitches beat against each other until they line up.",cites:["s4","s1"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"vibrato",name:"Vibrato",family:"Lead",level:"Beginner",instr:"guitar",summary:"Vibrato is a steady wobble in pitch that makes a held note sing. Blues and rock players get it by bending the string slightly back and forth across the fret; classical players rock the string along its length for a subtler effect.",how:["Sound the note first, then start the motion, so the listener hears the true pitch before it moves.","For blues and rock vibrato, drive the motion from the wrist. Keep the fretting finger firm and still, anchor the thumb on the neck, and rotate the wrist so it makes a stream of tiny bends. This is the approach associated with B.B. King.","For classical vibrato, rock the fingertip toward the headstock and toward the soundhole without pushing the string sideways. The pitch moves less, which suits the classical guitar's long, singing notes.","Choose speed and width for the style: slow and wide sounds like blues, fast and narrow sounds more intense and rock, and a whole-arm vibrato (Clapton, Hendrix) gives a big, dramatic wobble. Faster vibrato adds tension; slower sounds more lyrical.","Practise vibrato with every finger. On the third finger, add the index and middle fingers behind it for support."],mistakes:["Shaking the finger alone, which gives a weak, nervous sound. Lock the finger and let the wrist rotate.","Going so wide that the note sounds out of tune. Width should suit the music.","Changing speed suddenly with no steady pulse. Keep the wobble even, almost in time with the song.","Playing on the very tip of the finger. Use the pad, and add supporting fingers for strength."],used:"Every lead style. B.B. King's slow, wide wrist vibrato is the classic blues sound; Eric Clapton and Jimi Hendrix used whole-arm vibrato in rock; some heavy metal players go a whole tone wide or more; classical guitarists use subtle along-the-string vibrato on long notes.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Shake It Off: Everything You Need to Know About Vibrato",author:"Sean Weaver",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2017,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/shake-it-off-everything-you-need-to-know-about-vibrato",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"Finger vibrato",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finger_vibrato",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s3",title:"Basic Vibrato",site:"Andy Guitar",url:"https://www.andyguitar.co.uk/videos/08-int-elec-l2-basic-vibrato",kind:"video"},{id:"s4",title:"Vibrato",author:"Mark Wingfield",site:"markwingfield.com",url:"https://www.markwingfield.com/vibrato/",kind:"article"}],study:[{title:"The Art of Vibrato",kind:"article",url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/the-art-of-vibrato",note:"Another Premier Guitar look at how famous players shape their vibrato."},{title:"Improve your vibrato in 20 minutes",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.musicradar.com/how-to/improve-your-vibrato-in-20-minutes",note:"A short, focused practice routine."},{title:"B.B. King – Live at the Regal",kind:"album",note:"Close listening to his held notes is the best vibrato lesson there is."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"vibrato-held-notes",title:"Held notes with each finger",instr:"guitar",bpm:66,grid:4,notes:[[0,1,5,8,"v"],[8,1,8,8,"v"],[16,2,8,8,"v"],[24,4,7,8,"v"]],note:"Index on the A, little finger on the C, ring finger on the G and the low A. Let each note sound clean for a moment, then start an even, slow wobble.",cites:["s1","s3"]},{type:"tab",id:"vibrato-blues-phrase",title:"Blues phrase: bend, then vibrato",instr:"guitar",bpm:72,grid:4,notes:[[0,1,8,2],[2,1,5,2],[4,2,8,8,"b2,v"],[12,2,5,4,"v"],[16,3,7,2],[18,3,5,2],[20,4,7,12,"v"]],note:"Vibrato on top of a whole-step bend is harder: keep the bend up at pitch and let the wobble dip below it and back. Try it slow and wide, then fast and narrow, and hear how the mood changes.",cites:["s1","s2","s3"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"slides",name:"Slides",family:"Lead",level:"Beginner",instr:"guitar",summary:"A slide moves a fretted note to another fret without lifting the finger, so the pitch glides between them. You can pick only the first note (legato slide), pick both (shift slide), or slide in and out of a note from no fixed starting or ending point.",how:["Legato slide: pick the first note, keep the finger pressed, and glide to the second note without picking again.","Shift slide: pick the first note, glide to the second, and pick again as you land. The second note gets a clearer attack.","Slide-in: start a few frets below (or above) the target with no fixed starting note and glide into it, like a quick ornament.","Slide-off: pick a note, then glide down (or up) the neck while easing the pressure, with no fixed landing point. Slides in and out usually cover only a few frets.","Use just enough pressure to keep the string ringing. Too little and it buzzes or dies; too much and the note bends sharp. Know where you are going before you move."],mistakes:["Lifting the finger in the middle of the slide, which cuts the sound off.","Overshooting the target fret. Look at the landing fret and slow down until you hit it every time.","Pressing so hard that the string bends out of tune as it moves."],used:"Lead lines in every style. Slides are a natural way to change position on the neck in the middle of a phrase, and shift slides are especially common for that in blues playing. Legato slides sit alongside hammer-ons and pull-offs in smooth, flowing solos.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Guitar/Slides",site:"Wikibooks",url:"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Guitar/Slides",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"Slides (Guitar Pro 8 user guide)",site:"Guitar Pro",url:"https://www.guitar-pro.com/docs/gp8/playeffects/touch-effects/slides",kind:"article"},{id:"s3",title:"How to Play Slides on Guitar for Smoother, More Musical Lead Playing",site:"JG Music Lessons",url:"https://jgmusiclessons.com/how-to-play-slides-on-guitar/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"Improvements to slides in tablature",site:"Soundslice",url:"https://www.soundslice.com/blog/139/improvements-to-slides-in-tablature/",kind:"article"},{id:"s5",title:"Blues Slide Chords Lesson",site:"GuitarMasterClass",url:"https://www.guitarmasterclass.net/ls/Blues-Slide-Chords",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Learning to Slide: How This Legato Technique Can Enhance Your Sound",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/learning-slide-how-legato-technique-can-enhance-your-sound",note:"More slide licks in a rock and blues context."},{title:"Sliding technique crash course",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.merriammusic.com/blog/music-school/sliding-technique-crash-course/",note:"A short, beginner-friendly walk through the slide types."},{title:"Slides, Hammer-Ons & Pull-Offs",author:"Doug Boduch",kind:"video",url:"https://guitarworld.guitarinstructor.com/product/g-plus/doug-boduch/slides-hammer-ons-pull-offs/1000205377",note:"Puts slides together with the other legato moves."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"slides-legato-and-shift",title:"Legato slides up, shift slides down",instr:"guitar",bpm:80,grid:4,notes:[[0,3,7,2,"D"],[2,3,9,2,"s"],[4,2,8,2,"D"],[6,2,10,2,"s"],[8,1,8,2,"D"],[10,1,10,2,"s"],[12,1,12,4,"s"],[16,1,12,2,"D"],[18,1,10,2,"s,U"],[20,2,10,2,"D"],[22,2,8,2,"s,U"],[24,3,9,2,"D"],[26,3,7,2,"s,U"],[28,4,7,4,"D,v"]],note:"A minor pentatonic. In bar 1 only the first note of each pair is picked; in bar 2 the pick hits the landing note too (the U strokes).",cites:["s1","s2","s4"]},{type:"tab",id:"slides-in-and-off",title:"Slide-ins and slide-offs",instr:"guitar",bpm:84,grid:4,notes:[[0,2,3,1],[1,2,5,3,"s"],[4,1,5,2],[6,2,5,2],[8,3,7,2],[10,3,5,2],[12,4,7,4,"gl"],[16,1,10,1],[17,1,12,3,"s"],[20,2,13,2],[22,2,12,2],[24,2,10,8,"gl"]],note:"The quick first note of each bar is only a launch point for the slide-in. Each phrase ends with a slide-off: ease the pressure as the note falls away.",cites:["s1","s2","s3"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"tremolo-picking",name:"Tremolo picking",family:"Lead",level:"Intermediate",instr:"guitar",summary:"Tremolo picking is very fast, continuous alternate picking on one note at a time, so a melody seems to shimmer and sustain. It is the engine of surf lead guitar and of black metal melodies.",how:["Alternate down and up in a small, even motion. In its purest form the speed is not tied to a note value: you pick as fast as you can keep perfectly steady. In black metal it is often locked to steady sixteenth notes.","Let the picking hand float above the strings with no anchor. Some players lock the wrist and let the motion come from further up the arm, a style sometimes called 'hummingbird picking'.","Keep the pick going while the fretting hand changes notes, and land each new note exactly on a beat.","Build speed with a metronome: start around 55 bpm and raise it one click a minute toward 180 bpm.","For surf, keep the tone clean but big and add plenty of reverb, the sound surf players use to evoke waves."],mistakes:["Pushing past the speed you can keep steady, so the picking becomes a ragged blur. Back off until every stroke is even.","Fretting hand and picking hand drifting apart, so note changes land between beats. Count the strokes per note at a slow tempo first.","Big, tense strokes that tire you in seconds. Keep the motion small and the hand floating."],used:"Surf rock, led by Dick Dale, 'the King of Surf Guitar', whose 'Misirlou' is the classic tremolo-picked lead. Black metal guitarists tremolo pick whole arpeggios and scale runs in sixteenth notes. Mandolin players use the same idea, and Eddie Van Halen used fast single-note picking in 'Eruption'.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Surf Guitar Basics",author:"Gary Hoey",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2007,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/articles/surf-guitar-basics-1",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"Tremolo Picking on the Guitar",author:"Desi Serna",site:"Dummies",url:"https://www.dummies.com/article/academics-the-arts/music/instruments/guitar/tremolo-picking-on-the-guitar-143484/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s3",title:"Alternate picking",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternate_picking",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s4",title:"Master tremolo picking on guitar: from surf to black metal",site:"Guitar World",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/getting-started-tremolo-picking",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Dick Dale and His Del-Tones – Misirlou",kind:"album",note:"Listen to how steady tremolo picking turns a melody into one sustained, shimmering line."},{title:"How to master tremolo picking",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/techniques/how-to-master-tremolo-picking",note:"More exercises for building stamina and synchronisation."},{title:"Fast guitar picking",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.fretjam.com/fast-guitar-picking.html",note:"General speed-building advice that applies directly to tremolo picking."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"tremolo-picking-pulse",title:"One note: eighths to sixteenths",instr:"guitar",bpm:100,grid:4,notes:[[0,2,5,2,"D"],[2,2,5,2,"U"],[4,2,5,2,"D"],[6,2,5,2,"U"],[8,2,5,2,"D"],[10,2,5,2,"U"],[12,2,5,2,"D"],[14,2,5,2,"U"],[16,2,5,1,"D"],[17,2,5,1,"U"],[18,2,5,1,"D"],[19,2,5,1,"U"],[20,2,5,1,"D"],[21,2,5,1,"U"],[22,2,5,1,"D"],[23,2,5,1,"U"],[24,2,5,1,"D"],[25,2,5,1,"U"],[26,2,5,1,"D"],[27,2,5,1,"U"],[28,2,5,1,"D"],[29,2,5,1,"U"],[30,2,5,1,"D"],[31,2,5,1,"U"]],note:"Find a small, relaxed motion at eighth notes, then double it without making the motion bigger. Raise the tempo a click at a time.",cites:["s1","s2"]},{type:"tab",id:"tremolo-picking-surf",title:"Surf-style melody",instr:"guitar",bpm:132,grid:4,notes:[[0,1,12,1,"D"],[1,1,12,1,"U"],[2,1,12,1,"D"],[3,1,12,1,"U"],[4,1,12,1,"D"],[5,1,12,1,"U"],[6,1,12,1,"D"],[7,1,12,1,"U"],[8,1,10,1,"D"],[9,1,10,1,"U"],[10,1,10,1,"D"],[11,1,10,1,"U"],[12,1,8,1,"D"],[13,1,8,1,"U"],[14,1,8,1,"D"],[15,1,8,1,"U"],[16,1,7,1,"D"],[17,1,7,1,"U"],[18,1,7,1,"D"],[19,1,7,1,"U"],[20,2,9,1,"D"],[21,2,9,1,"U"],[22,2,9,1,"D"],[23,2,9,1,"U"],[24,2,10,1,"D"],[25,2,10,1,"U"],[26,2,10,1,"D"],[27,2,10,1,"U"],[28,2,10,1,"D"],[29,2,10,1,"U"],[30,2,10,1,"D"],[31,2,10,1,"U"]],note:"An A harmonic minor line (the G sharp gives it the exotic surf colour). Play it clean with lots of reverb and keep the picking unbroken across the string change.",cites:["s1","s2"]},{type:"tab",id:"tremolo-picking-black-metal",title:"Black metal style melody",instr:"guitar",bpm:150,grid:4,notes:[[0,1,12,1,"D"],[1,1,12,1,"U"],[2,1,12,1,"D"],[3,1,12,1,"U"],[4,1,12,1,"D"],[5,1,12,1,"U"],[6,1,12,1,"D"],[7,1,12,1,"U"],[8,1,10,1,"D"],[9,1,10,1,"U"],[10,1,10,1,"D"],[11,1,10,1,"U"],[12,1,7,1,"D"],[13,1,7,1,"U"],[14,1,7,1,"D"],[15,1,7,1,"U"],[16,1,8,1,"D"],[17,1,8,1,"U"],[18,1,8,1,"D"],[19,1,8,1,"U"],[20,1,8,1,"D"],[21,1,8,1,"U"],[22,1,8,1,"D"],[23,1,8,1,"U"],[24,1,7,1,"D"],[25,1,7,1,"U"],[26,1,7,1,"D"],[27,1,7,1,"U"],[28,1,7,1,"D"],[29,1,7,1,"U"],[30,1,7,1,"D"],[31,1,7,1,"U"]],note:"Strict sixteenths in E minor over an implied Em to C change. The melody moves in long notes while the pick never stops; the half-step fall from C to B carries the dark mood.",cites:["s3","s4"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"pinch-harmonics",name:"Pinch harmonics",family:"Lead",level:"Intermediate",instr:"guitar",summary:"A pinch harmonic happens when the side of your picking thumb catches the string just after the pick strikes it. That cancels the main pitch and lets a high overtone ring out as a squeal, which is easiest to hear on an electric guitar with some gain.",how:["Choke up on the pick so only a small tip shows between your thumb and index finger.","Pick with a slight forward twist so the flesh on the side of your thumb brushes the string right after the pick. Let the pick drag through the string.","Move your picking hand along the string, around the pickups, to find the sweet spots: each position brings out a different harmonic. Billy Gibbons starts near the bridge pickup and moves toward the neck for variety.","Turn the gain up while you learn; plenty of distortion helps the harmonic read.","Add vibrato once the harmonic speaks. Wide vibrato and plenty of distortion are central to the Zakk Wylde sound."],mistakes:["Showing too much pick, so the thumb never touches the string. Choke up until barely any tip shows.","Picking in one spot and giving up. The sweet spot moves with every fret, so slide your hand around.","Learning on a clean sound. Start with gain, then try backing it off once the motion is reliable.","Letting a bend drift out of tune when you add a harmonic to it. The bend still has to land on pitch."],used:"Blues-rock and metal. Billy Gibbons of ZZ Top made squeals part of his style, and Zakk Wylde has said his came from learning ZZ Top's 'La Grange' with his teacher; Wylde is probably the player best known for them. Robbie Robertson added them to bent notes.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Playing Pinch Harmonics",author:"Nick Kellie",site:"JamPlay",year:2016,url:"https://jamplay.com/articles/5-guides/66-playing-pinch-harmonics",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"How to Play Pinch Harmonics on Electric Guitar",author:"Jon Chappell",site:"Dummies (Blues Guitar For Dummies)",url:"https://www.dummies.com/article/academics-the-arts/music/instruments/guitar/how-to-play-pinch-harmonics-on-electric-guitar-198158/",kind:"book"},{id:"s3",title:"Zakk Wylde: ZZ Top inspired my use of pinch harmonics",author:"Joe DiVita",site:"Loudwire",year:2018,url:"https://loudwire.com/zakk-wylde-zz-top-inspiration-use-pinch-harmonics/",kind:"interview"},{id:"s4",title:"String harmonic",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/String_harmonic",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s5",title:"30-day guitar challenge, day 28: pinched harmonics masterclass",site:"MusicRadar",url:"https://www.musicradar.com/tuition/guitars/30-day-guitar-challenge-day-28-pinched-harmonics-masterclass-626704",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Pinch harmonics made easy: how to get the perfect squeal",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/techniques/pinch-harmonics",note:"A step-by-step lesson with more licks to practise."},{title:"ZZ Top – 'La Grange'",kind:"album",note:"The song Zakk Wylde credits as the source of his pinch harmonics."},{title:"Pinch harmonics: jaw-dropping guitar screams",kind:"article",url:"https://www.gearnews.com/pinch-harmonics-jaw-dropping-guitar-screams/",note:"Covers the gear side: pickups, gain and string choice."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"pinch-harmonics-find-it",title:"Find the sweet spot",instr:"guitar",bpm:70,grid:4,notes:[[0,3,7,4,"D,ph"],[4,3,7,4,"D,ph"],[8,3,7,8,"D,ph,v"],[16,2,8,4,"D,ph"],[20,2,8,4,"D,ph"],[24,2,8,8,"D,ph,v"]],note:"Same note, picked again and again. Move the picking hand a little each time until the squeal jumps out, then add vibrato.",cites:["s1","s2"]},{type:"tab",id:"pinch-harmonics-blues-lick",title:"Blues-rock lick with squeals",instr:"guitar",bpm:80,grid:4,notes:[[0,2,8,2,"D"],[2,2,5,2,"D"],[4,3,7,8,"D,ph,b2,v"],[12,3,5,2,"D"],[14,4,7,2,"D"],[16,3,5,2,"D"],[18,4,7,2,"D"],[20,4,5,4,"D,ph,v"],[24,4,7,8,"D,ph,v"]],note:"A minor pentatonic. The squeal on the bend is the hard part: catch the harmonic first, then bend it up a whole step in tune.",cites:["s1","s3"]},{type:"tab",id:"pinch-harmonics-metal-riff",title:"Metal riff with squeals",instr:"guitar",bpm:100,grid:4,notes:[[0,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[1,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[2,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[3,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[4,3,4,4,"D,ph,v"],[8,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[9,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[10,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[11,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[12,4,2,4,"D,ph,v"],[16,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[17,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[18,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[19,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[20,4,5,4,"D,ph,b2"],[24,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[25,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[26,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[27,6,0,1,"D,pm"],[28,3,4,4,"D,ph,v"]],note:"Palm-muted low E chugs answered by squealing notes in E minor. Lift the palm mute for the squeals so they can ring.",cites:["s4","s5"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"natural-harmonics",name:"Natural harmonics",family:"Harmony",level:"Beginner",instr:"guitar",summary:"A natural harmonic is a bell-like note you get by lightly touching an open string at a node, such as the 12th, 7th or 5th fret, as you pick it. The touch makes the string vibrate in equal sections, so it sounds one of its higher overtones.",how:["Lay a fretting finger lightly on the string directly over the fret wire, touching it by a millimetre or two without pressing it down. The side of the finger often works better than a hard fingertip.","Pick the string, then lift the touching finger straight away so the harmonic can ring.","Pick closer to the bridge than you normally would; harmonics speak more clearly there.","Start at the 12th fret, which halves the string and sounds an octave above the open string. Then try the 7th fret (the string in thirds: an octave and a fifth up) and the 5th fret (the string in quarters: two octaves up).","The 4th-fret harmonic (two octaves and a major third up) is fainter and needs an even lighter, more precise touch. Gain helps on electric guitar."],mistakes:["Pressing too hard, which mutes the harmonic, or too lightly, which lets the open string sound through.","Touching the string off to one side of the fret wire. Aim directly over it.","Leaving the finger on the string after picking, which chokes the note.","Picking in your usual spot over the soundhole or neck pickup. Move the pick toward the bridge."],used:"Steve Howe opens Yes's 'Roundabout' with a 12th-fret harmonic, western swing guitarists use harmonics, and classical guitarists play single harmonics and whole harmonic chords. In metal, Dimebag Darrell combined natural harmonics with the whammy bar. Harmonics also spell out arpeggios and chords high above the normal range of the guitar.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"How to Play Natural Harmonics on the Guitar",author:"Desi Serna",site:"Dummies",url:"https://www.dummies.com/article/academics-the-arts/music/instruments/guitar/how-to-play-natural-harmonics-on-the-guitar-143490",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"How to Play Guitar Harmonics Like a Pro",site:"TrueFire",url:"https://truefire.com/learn/how-to-play-guitar-harmonics",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s3",title:"Guitar Technique Tip of the Month: Harmonics",author:"Douglas Niedt",site:"douglasniedt.com",url:"https://douglasniedt.com/Tech_Tip_Harmonics.html",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"String harmonic",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/String_harmonic",kind:"encyclopedia"}],study:[{title:"Yes – 'Roundabout' (from Fragile)",kind:"album",note:"A famous harmonic intro to hear the 12th-fret sound in context."},{title:"Harmonics (Natural and Artificial)",kind:"article",url:"https://cgsmusic.net/dictionary/term/harmonics-natural-and-artificial/",note:"A short reference on how natural and artificial harmonics differ."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"natural-harmonics-twelfth",title:"12th-fret harmonics across the strings",instr:"guitar",bpm:72,grid:2,ring:!0,notes:[[0,6,12,8,"nh"],[1,5,12,7,"nh"],[2,4,12,6,"nh"],[3,3,12,5,"nh"],[4,2,12,4,"nh"],[5,1,12,3,"nh"],[8,3,12,8,"nh"],[8,2,12,8,"nh"],[8,1,12,8,"nh"]],note:"Each harmonic sounds an octave above its open string. Let them ring together, then strum the top three for an E minor chime.",cites:["s1","s2"]},{type:"tab",id:"natural-harmonics-twelve-seven-five",title:"Chimes at the 12th, 7th and 5th frets",instr:"guitar",bpm:72,grid:2,ring:!0,notes:[[0,3,12,3,"nh"],[1,2,12,3,"nh"],[2,1,12,3,"nh"],[3,3,7,2,"nh"],[4,2,7,2,"nh"],[5,1,7,3,"nh"],[6,3,5,2,"nh"],[8,4,5,4,"nh"],[8,3,5,4,"nh"],[8,2,5,4,"nh"],[12,4,7,4,"nh"],[12,3,7,4,"nh"],[12,2,7,4,"nh"],[16,4,12,8,"nh"],[16,3,12,8,"nh"],[16,2,12,8,"nh"]],note:"Bar 1 rings out a G major colour from all three nodes. Bars 2 and 3 strum G, D and G chords made only of harmonics on the D, G and B strings.",cites:["s2","s4"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"string-skipping",name:"String skipping",family:"Lead",level:"Intermediate",instr:"guitar",summary:"String skipping means jumping over one or more strings on the way to the next note. It puts wide intervals into lines and arpeggios, so they sound less like familiar guitar fingerings.",how:["Start with a pentatonic shape you know and play it in pairs of strings with one string between them.","Keep the picking motion small and economical. The pick has to hop over the skipped string, so accuracy matters more than force.","Try different picking choices (strict alternate, outside picking, economy picking) and keep the one that sounds even and feels economical to you.","Mute carefully with the fretting hand. Because the picking hand jumps over strings, stray string noise is harder to control than in adjacent-string playing.","For arpeggios, put two notes on one string and one or two on a string two away. The notes stay separate and defined, where adjacent-string sweeps can blur together.","Practise the jumps slowly and in strict time before adding speed."],mistakes:["Hitting the skipped string on the way past. Keep the pick close to the strings and the jump small.","Ringing open strings and noise between notes. Use the fretting hand to damp every string you are not playing.","Rushing the jumps so the timing wobbles. Slow down until each skip lands on the grid."],used:"Rock and metal lead playing. Paul Gilbert is famous for string-skipped arpeggios, Eric Johnson uses skips in the intro of 'Cliffs of Dover', and Shawn Lane, Nuno Bettencourt and Guthrie Govan use it widely. The intro of Guns N' Roses' 'Sweet Child o' Mine' is a well-known example.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Cram Session: The Ups and Downs of String Skipping",author:"Jamie Humphries",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2016,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/cram-session-the-ups-and-downs-of-string-skipping",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"String skipping",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/String_skipping",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s3",title:"String Skipping: Sequences for Wide-Interval Guitar Shredding",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2010,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/string-skipping-sequences-for-wide-interval-guitar-shredding",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"Alternate Picking Lesson",site:"fretjam",url:"https://fretjam.com/alternate-picking.html",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Paul Gilbert-style sequenced string-skipping arpeggios",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarworld.com/lessons/guitar-strength-some-paul-gilbert-style-sequenced-string-skipping-arpeggios",note:"Develops the arpeggio idea from exercise 2 into Gilbert's sequences."},{title:"Paul Gilbert Gives a Classic Lesson in Shred",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.guitarplayer.com/technique/paul-gilbert-gives-a-classic-lesson-in-shred-tab-audio",note:"Gilbert explaining his own wide-interval approach, with tab and audio."},{title:"Eric Johnson – Ah Via Musicom",kind:"album",note:"Contains 'Cliffs of Dover', a famous string-skipping intro."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"string-skipping-pentatonic",title:"Pentatonic in skipped pairs",instr:"guitar",bpm:70,grid:4,notes:[[0,6,5,1,"D"],[1,4,5,1,"U"],[2,6,8,1,"D"],[3,4,7,1,"U"],[4,5,5,1,"D"],[5,3,5,1,"U"],[6,5,7,1,"D"],[7,3,7,1,"U"],[8,4,5,1,"D"],[9,2,5,1,"U"],[10,4,7,1,"D"],[11,2,8,1,"U"],[12,3,5,1,"D"],[13,1,5,1,"U"],[14,3,7,1,"D"],[15,1,8,1,"U"],[16,1,8,1,"D"],[17,3,7,1,"U"],[18,1,5,1,"D"],[19,3,5,1,"U"],[20,2,8,1,"D"],[21,4,7,1,"U"],[22,2,5,1,"D"],[23,4,5,1,"U"],[24,3,7,1,"D"],[25,5,7,1,"U"],[26,3,5,1,"D"],[27,5,5,1,"U"],[28,4,7,1,"D"],[29,6,8,1,"U"],[30,4,5,1,"D"],[31,6,5,1,"U"],[32,4,7,8,"D,v"]],note:"The A minor pentatonic box, played so every pair of notes jumps over a string. Every skip is down then up, so the pick hops the gap on each stroke.",cites:["s1","s3"]},{type:"tab",id:"string-skipping-arpeggios",title:"Skipped arpeggios: Am, G, F, E",instr:"guitar",bpm:80,grid:3,notes:[[0,3,5,1,"D"],[1,3,9,1,"U"],[2,1,5,1,"D"],[3,1,8,1,"U"],[4,1,5,1,"D"],[5,3,9,1,"U"],[6,3,4,1,"D"],[7,3,7,1,"U"],[8,1,3,1,"D"],[9,1,7,1,"U"],[10,1,3,1,"D"],[11,3,7,1,"U"],[12,3,2,1,"D"],[13,3,5,1,"U"],[14,1,1,1,"D"],[15,1,5,1,"U"],[16,1,1,1,"D"],[17,3,5,1,"U"],[18,3,1,1,"D"],[19,3,4,1,"U"],[20,1,0,1,"D"],[21,1,4,1,"U"],[22,1,0,1,"D"],[23,3,4,1,"U"],[24,3,2,12,"D,v"]],note:"Each triad uses the G and high E strings only, skipping the B string. Mute the B string with the underside of your fretting fingers.",cites:["s1","s2"]}]}),LEARN.technique({id:"double-stops",name:"Double stops",family:"Lead",level:"Beginner",instr:"guitar",summary:"A double stop is two notes played at once. Thirds and sixths are the most common pairs in country guitar, and sixths are a signature sound in the short, punchy fills of soul and R&B.",how:["Learn thirds on the G and B strings as a moving shape that climbs the major scale. Some pairs are major thirds and some are minor; let the key decide, and use your ear as much as the chart.","Find sixths by dropping the middle note of a triad shape on the G, B and E strings. What is left is a sixth on the G and E strings, with the B string muted under your fretting fingers.","Pick both notes together, or use hybrid picking (pick plus middle finger) or thumb and finger, which lets you balance the two notes and play non-adjacent strings cleanly.","Add movement: slide into a shape from a fret below, or pick the pair with the lower note a half step flat and hammer it up. Hammered double stops are a signature Stax soul sound.","Match the attack to the style: snappy and slightly muted for country, short and staccato for soul."],mistakes:["Letting one note of the pair swamp the other. Hybrid picking or fingers let you balance the two, so try a lick with the pick and then with fingers alone.","Treating double stops as random shapes. Each one comes from a chord shape, so connect it to the chord underneath.","Long, ringing notes where the style wants short stabs. In soul especially, lift the fretting fingers to stop each double stop."],used:"Country players lean on thirds and sixths more than any other pairs. Soul guitarists, Steve Cropper at Stax above all, built fills from sixths and hammered double stops. Funk, R&B and gospel players use small two- and three-note clusters like a horn section, and rock, blues, metal and jazz players use double stops too; Jimi Hendrix was especially known for them.",sources:[{id:"s1",title:"Double-Stops for Days",author:"Matthew Lee",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2021,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/country/country-double-stops",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s2",title:"Playing Soul Guitar Rhythm",author:"Shawn Persinger",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2023,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/articles/27845-sweet-soul-music",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s3",title:"Stoppin' on a Dime: Using Double- and Triple-Stops to Enhance Your Grooves",author:"Mark Lettieri",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2018,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/stoppin-on-a-dime-using-double-and-triple-stops-to-enhance-your-grooves",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s4",title:"Country Guitar Lesson: Two-Note Harmonizing",site:"ArtistWorks",year:2023,url:"https://artistworks.com/blog/country-guitar-lesson-double-stops",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s5",title:"How To Play Guitar Double-Stops",author:"Robbie Calvo",site:"Yamaha Music",year:2022,url:"https://hub.yamaha.com/?p=50528",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s6",title:"Double stop",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_stop",kind:"encyclopedia"}],study:[{title:"Electric double stops: 3rds and 6ths",kind:"video",url:"https://www.andyguitar.co.uk/videos/lesson-6-electric-double-stops-3rds-6ths",note:"A video lesson on both intervals with hybrid picking."},{title:"Top Steve Cropper Riffs: Soul Man",kind:"lesson",url:"https://www.licklibrary.com/learn/lessons/steve-cropper/top-steve-cropper-riffs-soul-man",note:"Shows how Cropper's double stops sit inside a classic soul arrangement."},{title:"Double stop guitar lesson: learning how to play harmonizing lead",kind:"lesson",url:"https://activemelody.com/lesson/double-stop-guitar-lesson-learning-how-to-play-harmonizing-lead-ep055",note:"Connects double-stop shapes to the chord shapes you already know."}],cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5","s6"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"double-stops-thirds",title:"Country thirds in A",instr:"guitar",bpm:88,grid:4,notes:[[0,3,5,1,"D"],[0,2,5,4,"D"],[1,3,6,3,"h"],[4,3,7,2,"D"],[4,2,7,2,"D"],[6,3,9,2,"D"],[6,2,9,2,"D"],[8,3,11,2,"D"],[8,2,10,2,"D"],[10,3,9,2,"D"],[10,2,9,2,"D"],[12,3,7,2,"D"],[12,2,7,2,"D"],[14,3,6,2,"D"],[14,2,5,2,"D"],[16,3,7,2,"D"],[16,2,7,2,"D"],[18,3,9,2,"s"],[18,2,9,2,"s"],[20,3,7,2,"D"],[20,2,7,2,"D"],[22,3,6,2,"D"],[22,2,5,2,"D"],[24,3,2,8,"D"],[24,2,2,8,"D"]],note:"Thirds on the G and B strings climbing the A major scale. It opens with a bluesy hammer from C to C sharp and slides both notes in bar 2.",cites:["s1","s4","s5"]},{type:"tab",id:"double-stops-sixths",title:"Soul sixths in C",instr:"guitar",bpm:92,grid:4,notes:[[0,3,8,1,"D"],[0,1,8,4,"D"],[1,3,9,3,"h"],[6,3,9,2,"U"],[6,1,8,2,"U"],[8,3,4,1,"D"],[8,1,5,4,"D"],[9,3,5,3,"h"],[14,3,5,2,"U"],[14,1,5,2,"U"],[16,3,8,1,"D"],[16,1,8,4,"D"],[17,3,9,3,"h"],[20,3,7,2,"D"],[20,1,7,2,"D"],[22,3,5,2,"D"],[22,1,5,2,"D"],[24,3,4,2,"D"],[24,1,3,2,"D"],[26,3,2,2,"D"],[26,1,1,2,"D"],[28,3,0,4,"D"],[28,1,0,4,"D"]],note:"Sixths on the G and high E strings with the B string muted. Hammered sixths over C and F, then a walk down the C major scale in sixths; keep every stab short.",cites:["s2","s3"]}]});